{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdL7JYabzjEaoyVaEHJR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LiSB4KXkRXeHwowu7VsAtD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"C=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_4_repair_1; cd $C\ncat > hypothesis.md << 'EOF'\nRefinement of the incumbent `asymmetric_tolerance_lopsided_stretch`: people still find a sequence less random when one stretch of it shows a striking local heads/tails excess, and still judge it by their own ideal switching rate and sensitivity (switching too often costing only a shared fraction of what switching too rarely costs), personal weights on overall imbalance and the longest streak, shared weights on the final streak, mirror symmetry and run-length variety, and a personal guessing rate. The one change is how the local excess is sized: people notice it in absolute flips — a surplus of three heads in one stretch is equally striking whether the sequence has four flips or eight — rather than as a share of the sequence's length, so the local-excess penalty bites harder in long sequences and much less in short ones than the incumbent's length-normalised cue implies.\nEOF\necho absolute_local_excess_aversion > model_name.txt\npython3 - << 'EOF'\np='candidate.py'\ns=open('/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/models/asymmetric_tolerance_lopsided_stretch.py').read()\ndoc_old=s[:s.index('import numpy')]\ndoc='''\"\"\"Absolute-size local excess on the asymmetric-tolerance lopsided-stretch model.\n\nRefinement of `asymmetric_tolerance_lopsided_stretch`: one striking local\nheads/tails excess still makes a sequence look designed, on top of the personal\nideal switching rate and sensitivity (with a shared, lenient fraction for\nover-alternation), personal weights on imbalance and the longest run, shared\nwei